From: steamlink-ps5
Subject: channel: send ACKs several times so reliable control survives a lossy uplink

On a weak Wi-Fi link the console's ACKs are lost on the way back to the host,
so the host retransmits each reliable control message many times and then gives
up. That silently breaks the multi-step control handshakes: the host opens the
HID pad but never starts input reports (so the controller does nothing), and
keyframe/config delivery stalls (so video freezes for many seconds). An ACK is
a tiny idempotent packet; send it three times so at least one gets back even
when the uplink is dropping packets. NACKs stay single.

diff -ru a/src/session/channels/channel.c b/src/session/channels/channel.c
--- a/src/session/channels/channel.c	2026-10-08 17:01:34.254697003 -0700
+++ b/src/session/channels/channel.c	2026-10-09 11:11:12.736324038 -0700
@@ -198,12 +198,23 @@
 }
 
 void IHS_SessionChannelPacketAck(IHS_SessionChannel *channel, int32_t packetId, bool ok) {
-    IHS_SessionPacket packet;
     IHS_SessionPacketType type = ok ? IHS_SessionPacketTypeACK : IHS_SessionPacketTypeNACK;
-    IHS_SessionChannelInitializePacket(channel, &packet, type, true, packetId);
-    IHS_BufferAppendUInt32LE(&packet.body, IHS_SessionPacketTimestamp());
-    IHS_SessionChannelQueuePacket(channel, &packet, false);
-    IHS_SessionPacketClear(&packet, true);
+    /*
+     * Send an ACK more than once. It is a ~60-byte packet and losing it on a
+     * weak uplink makes the host retransmit the whole reliable message and, after
+     * a handful of tries, give up - which silently breaks multi-step control
+     * handshakes (start input reports, keyframe delivery, config). ACKs are
+     * idempotent, so three copies cost almost nothing and make delivery robust.
+     * NACKs stay single so we do not ask for the same resend several times.
+     */
+    int copies = (type == IHS_SessionPacketTypeACK) ? 3 : 1;
+    for (int i = 0; i < copies; i++) {
+        IHS_SessionPacket packet;
+        IHS_SessionChannelInitializePacket(channel, &packet, type, true, packetId);
+        IHS_BufferAppendUInt32LE(&packet.body, IHS_SessionPacketTimestamp());
+        IHS_SessionChannelQueuePacket(channel, &packet, false);
+        IHS_SessionPacketClear(&packet, true);
+    }
 }
 
 static bool SessionChannelQueueFramePackets(IHS_SessionChannel *channel, IHS_SessionFrame *frame, size_t bodyLimit,
